WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.093 --> 00:00:03.240
- That Roe is dead opens more
questions than it answers.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.280
What happens next after
the Dobb's decision?

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.810
Well, today we're going to look at some

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.760
of the most pressing
issues from trigger laws

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.970
to abortion pills, to
federal codification of Roe.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.710
And we start with the idea

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.990
of whether states can
criminalize abortion.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.870
The answer here is
obviously yes they can now.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.820
The Supreme Court ruled
that states have the right

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.000
to regulate abortion
because abortion laws now

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.360
are subject to rational basis review,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.970
which means that as long as
they have any rational reason

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.200
for passing a law,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.020
it will pass, and that means that states

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.940
can now criminalize abortions.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.880
Before Roe vs. Wade, two-thirds
of states outlawed abortion

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.250
with some making it a criminal offense.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.200
The majority opinion in Dobbs says

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.570
that women do not have a
fundamental right to privacy

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:46.830
when it comes to abortions.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.560
And therefore, the opinion
took no official stance

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.000
on whether it was okay for states

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.220
to prosecute women
abortion service providers,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.010
or people who helped
women obtain an abortion

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.630
that will be left up to the state

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.340
and the states will be able

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.010
to criminalize that if they so desire.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.530
And obviously looking state
by state the situation

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.300
in many states is in flux.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.000
There are 27 states where
abortion is protected

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.680
and are likely to stay that way.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.750
Abortion protective
states are passing laws

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.150
to create safe havens
for people who travel

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.260
from out of state.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.790
Now, Connecticut passed a law saying

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.010
that its agencies will not participate

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.060
in any investigations,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.640
lawsuits, prosecutions,
or anything related

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.130
to abortions performed in Connecticut.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.600
The state residents will also be allowed

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.360
to countersue if they are
sued for offering help

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.330
to abortion patients in another state.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.550
This is in response to the
Texas bounty hunter law,

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.990
and New York, Illinois and California

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.580
are debating similar efforts.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.800
Of course, once states begin denying

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.540
each other's citizens certain rights,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.540
this invites more conflict
for the courts to decide.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.580
What about comity between the states

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.680
these interstate conflicts could redefine

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.620
how much sovereignty states
have in this federal assistant.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.270
And then there are the trigger laws.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.040
13 states had trigger laws

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.470
on the books that banned
abortion as soon as Roe and Casey

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.310
were overturned.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.570
These 13 states would trigger laws

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.510
had exceptions to save the
life of the pregnant woman,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:04.650
but only five of these states

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.630
have exceptions for rape or incest.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:07.680
And if someone becomes pregnant

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.060
by a male relative in Arkansas,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.670
Kentucky, Louisiana, Missouri,
North and South Dakota,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.890
Tennessee, Texas or Mississippi,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.450
they will be forced to give birth,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.100
and if someone is raped in those states,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.910
they will also be forced to give birth.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.230
- And I just have never
believed that having a tragedy

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.930
or a tragic situation happened to someone

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.520
is a reason to have another tragedy occur-

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.700
- So no exception for rape or incest?

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.230
- We continue to make sure we go forward.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.670
- There are five states including Texas

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.020
where abortion is banned,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.860
and there are eight states

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.420
where abortion will soon be banned.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.680
And there are of course a huge variety

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.520
of different pro-choice

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.320
and anti-abortion laws
throughout the country.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.790
Different states have different cut offs,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.270
some are based on viability
others on a number of weeks.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.950
Some allow exceptions for rape and incest

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.440
and some do not and it's only
going to get more complicated

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.530
as states rush to ban or allow abortion.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.990
But what about the fetal heartbeat bills?

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.520
Until the Dobb's ruling

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.320
federal courts had unanimously struck down

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.660
the so called heartbeat
bills outlawing abortion

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.610
after the detection of a fetal heartbeat.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.520
And that's why states like
Ohio, Georgia, South Carolina

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.120
and Iowa passed trigger laws
with six week abortion bans.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.400
Legislators did not repeal those
laws and abortion is banned

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.440
after the sixth week of
pregnancy in those states.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.210
The Supreme Court decision
did not say that states

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.100
had to allow abortion until week six

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.470
so any of those states are
free to pass total bans

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.580
if they desire though,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.580
the Georgia law is currently
enjoined by the court.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.240
And then of course,

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:26.100
much has been said about
potential religious exceptions.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.410
Many states have laws on the
books that allow pharmacists

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.210
to refuse to fill prescriptions,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.670
if they have moral or
religious objections.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.770
And with the Supreme Court's
ever increasing deference

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.510
to individual religious liberties,

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:37.650
we may see more pharmacists

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.210
and medical providers claiming a right

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.410
not to fill prescriptions

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.990
for abortion medication or
participate in services,

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.540
like surgery to remove
an ectopic pregnancy.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.670
And it's unclear how those
cases would be treated.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.550
It's similarly unclear how
the court would treat a claim

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:51.630
of religious liberty claiming

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.030
that based on certain religious beliefs,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.960
abortion is mandated in
certain circumstances,

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.060
even in a state that outlaws abortion.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.340
Who knows how the court is
going to come down on that.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.380
But it's also possible
continued jurisprudence

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.410
from this Supreme Court could
answer all of these questions

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.170
and the negative and
simply outlaw abortion.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.300
Constitutional rights currently
only attached to people

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.420
who are already born the
debate in Roe, Casey and Dobbs

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.040
was overbalancing a woman's right

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.570
to the state's interest.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.310
But anti-abortion activists argue

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.380
that individual rights
should attach to embryos

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.840
and fetus sometimes at
the point of conception,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.040
putting them on equal legal
grounds as a born human being.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.420
Now, the theory that unborn lives

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.150
or potential life are included
in the constitutional concept

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.480
of persons is not particularly developed

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.430
and the unborn have never been counted

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.410
in the US Census for example.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.120
They've also never been treated as persons

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.190
for purposes of constitutional rights.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.380
However, Justice Alito left the door open

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.680
to fetal personhood in the Dobb's opinion.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.160
In Roe versus Wade,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.260
the court said, "That the unborn
have never been recognized

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.330
in law as persons in the whole sense."

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.163
The Casey the court said,

00:04:55.163 --> 00:04:56.670
"That a woman has a
right to an abortion up

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.950
until the point in which
the fetus could be viable

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.390
and live outside the mother."

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.390
And even after that point,

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:03.223
there are medical situations

00:05:03.223 --> 00:05:04.920
where women would need an abortion

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.130
to save her life and
that would be allowed.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.150
But in a doctor's opinion,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.370
Justice Alito declined to
say exactly when rights

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.680
should attach to potential life,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.930
but he criticized Roe
and Casey for attempting

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.190
to draw that line.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.620
However, Alito himself
use the same terminology

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.030
that was used in Roe and Casey that

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.830
of potential human life

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.080
without agreeing with it,

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.070
which is often a linguistic
trick that signals

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.390
that he actually disagrees

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.070
with that conception of it.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.110
This Supreme Court is
already treading new ground

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.440
with respect to what
qualifies as deeply rooted

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:35.790
within the history of the United States

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.410
are necessary for ordered liberty.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.660
And is it entirely possible
that they might find

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.410
that Liberty interests or
personhood attaches to potential

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.090
or unborn life.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.830
Six states already have introduced fetal

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.990
personhood legislation in 2022.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.150
And the situation in Arizona
gives us a pretty good idea

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.220
of how complicated this issue may become.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.650
The state enacted a 15 week abortion ban,

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.570
but that was before the
doctor's opinion made it legal

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:58.830
to ban all abortions.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.260
Arizona simultaneously enacted a policy

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.420
that gives potential
life to full personhood.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.917
The state's interpretation
policy states the following:

00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:07.620
"The laws of the state
shall be interpreted

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:08.880
and construed to acknowledge,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.250
on behalf of an unborn
child at every stage

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.570
of development, all rights,

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.970
privileges and immunities
available to other persons,

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:16.680
citizens and residents of the state,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.900
subject only to the Constitution
of the United States

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.940
and decisions interpreting thereof

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.860
by the United States Supreme Court."

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.720
This interpretation policy incorporates

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:25.800
the statutory definition

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.920
of unborn child set forth in
Arizona law which provides

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.980
that an unborn child is "The
offspring of human beings

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.780
from conception until birth."

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.400
As written the law could impact a woman

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.600
throughout her pregnancy,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.730
giving the state the ability to regulate

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.700
how she handles basically
every aspect of that pregnancy.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.650
And as written, it could
encompass fertilized eggs

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.710
which would have the same
status as a born human being,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.670
triggering limitations on
abortion medications and even IVF.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.310
The ACLU of Arizona and the
Center for Reproductive Rights

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.200
sued to block implementation of this law,

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.090
and the court is scheduled
to have a hearing

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.470
on this lawsuit in July.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.331
But if the Supreme Court
adopted such a definition

00:07:00.331 --> 00:07:02.670
as being mandated by the Constitution,

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:06.420
we presumably prevent all
abortions in all 50 states.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.460
And that brings us to the
potential national provision

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.380
of abortion pills through the mail.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.810
Now over half of all abortions
in the United States are done

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.750
with medication which means
that they are induced by pills.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.600
In 2021, the Food and Drug
Administration the FDA decided

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.150
to allow abortion pills giving
providers the green light

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.800
to distribute them by mail.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.210
The medication which is used

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.590
during the first 10 weeks of pregnancy

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.150
is a two drug regimen.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.090
And the FDA removed a prior
guideline requiring the person

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.430
to meet personally with
a licensed physician.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.100
19 states banned the
administration of an abortion pill

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:36.660
by a telehealth provider,

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.700
even if that person is
a licensed physician.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:41.550
GenBioPro, the manufacturer
of the generic drug

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.410
sued the state of Mississippi back in May

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.067
after the state passed a law saying that,

00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:47.790
"Only a state licensed
physician could prescribe

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.230
the medication in person."

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.300
The state also requires
women to take the pills

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.190
in the presence of a physician.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.440
Now the pharmaceutical
company contends that the FDA

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.730
is regulations for dispensing

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.500
and prescribing the pill preempt

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.230
the state's requirements, and
generally a federal agencies

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.480
regulations have supremacy over state law.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.620
However, this is another area

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.300
where the Supreme Court is re-evaluating.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.760
This Supreme Court is
currently scaling back

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.980
regulatory authority and reinvigorating

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.080
the non-delegation doctrine,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.970
all of which could have
significant effects

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.430
on what the FDA can and cannot regulate.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.610
And a state is likely to
defend such lawsuits by arguing

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.760
that Congress did not give
the FDA explicit authority

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.950
to regulate abortion pills and thus,

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.620
there's nothing to override state laws.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.300
And then there's interstate travel.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.400
Will women be able to
travel to another state

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.600
to get an abortion?

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.600
Well, Justice Cavanaugh wrote
in his concurring opinion

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.760
that the state cannot
ban women from traveling

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.680
from one state to another
to get an abortion.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.080
He called that an easy question to answer.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:44.730
However, that issue
wasn't before the court

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:45.780
in the Dobb's case,

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.030
and his opinion doesn't
have any legal weight.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:48.863
So is he right?

00:08:48.863 --> 00:08:51.150
Well, there's actually not a
lot of case law on the right

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.380
to travel between states.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.310
During the Dust Bowl years,

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:54.780
California tried to ban immigration

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:56.070
of people from Oklahoma.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:56.903
At the time,

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:58.380
California asserted that mass migration

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.450
of indigenous people
stressed the state's budget

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.910
and created a crisis of health and morals.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.040
The states generally
have broad police power

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.020
to regulate health,
safety, welfare and morals

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:07.950
of its citizens,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.780
but the Supreme Court
struck down along the basis

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.370
that it was not a valid exercise

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:12.660
of the police power of California,

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.090
and that imposes an
unconstitutional burden

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.280
upon interstate commerce.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.780
In addition to the Commerce Clause,

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.240
people might look to the
Privileges and Immunities Clause

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:22.710
which protects the rights of US citizens

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.780
against discriminatory
treatment under the law

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:25.740
of a different state.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.900
And in a 1985 case
called the Supreme Court

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.840
of New Hampshire versus Piper,
the US Supreme Court held

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.120
that a New Hampshire rule
that limited bar admission

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.700
to state residents violated the Privileges

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.320
and Immunities Clause.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.000
In 1999, the court decided

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.480
another privileges immunities
case Saenz versus Roe.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.747
In that case, the court said,

00:09:43.747 --> 00:09:45.330
"That the right to
interstate travel consists

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:46.590
of three components.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:47.730
One, the right to enter

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:48.690
and leave another state,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.850
two, the right to be
treated as a welcome visitor

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.550
when temporarily present
in a another state,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.950
and three the right to be
treated like other citizens

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:57.360
of that state for those travelers

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.520
who would like to become
permanent residents.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.660
These cases generally say,

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:02.100
that people have a constitutional right

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.900
to move freely between states,

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.810
but states are asserting new
powers over their residence.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:08.520
We've covered the Senate Bill 8,

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:09.750
the Texas Bounty Hunter Law

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.190
that allows citizens to sue anyone

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.410
who assist someone in
obtaining an abortion.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.420
And since the Supreme Court
didn't strike that law down

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.810
other states were inspired
to draft similar laws.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.670
In Missouri lawmakers are debating a law

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.950
that would allow the state to
criminally prosecute anyone

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.800
who aid someone in getting
an abortion in Missouri,

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.320
but also if they help the
person traveled to a state

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.300
where the abortion is legal.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.310
And obviously, there are all
sorts of problems underlying

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:33.840
these proposed laws because it purports

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.670
to make a criminal
conspiracy out of conduct

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.530
that occurs wholly in another state,

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.510
where Missouri has no jurisdiction.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:42.990
And the Missouri legislators
who are drafting the bill

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.990
also wants a great civil
liability for anyone in the chain,

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.060
who helps the Missouri
resident get an abortion

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.070
including people who do the marketing

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.590
of an out of state abortion clinic.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:53.790
Though the Supreme Court has weighed

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:56.400
in on a similar situation before, in 1975,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.860
the Court reversed the
conviction of a Virginia editor

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.800
whose newspaper advertised
reproductive health services

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.207
offered in New York, the court said,

00:11:04.207 --> 00:11:05.850
"A state does not acquire the power

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:07.650
or supervision over the internal affairs

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.990
of another state merely
because the welfare

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:11.910
and health of its own
citizens may be affected

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.380
when they traveled to that state.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.840
Now, states that are protective
of reproductive rights,

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.030
like California and Massachusetts,

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.220
are also passing laws
welcoming non-residents

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.720
who want abortion services

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.850
and shielding their own
citizens from prosecution

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:24.930
and civil liability.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.790
For example, a Massachusetts law exempts

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:28.830
in state health care
providers from liability

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:31.110
for providing services to abortion seekers

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:32.250
from out of state.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.710
The law prohibits the state
from extraditing anyone

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:37.710
to a state that outlaws
abortion and protects people

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.350
from having their medical
records disclosed to states

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.840
that have criminal penalties for abortion.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.310
But just because the Supreme Court weighed

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.470
in on a similar question 45 years ago,

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.330
doesn't mean that the
current Supreme Court

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.130
is going to have the same outcome,

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.960
especially where we're
going to have even more

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.640
of a patchwork of different laws,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.940
some of which are trying
to create criminal guilt,

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.100
and some of which are
trying to prevent it.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.780
And then of course, there's
the First Amendment.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.000
Since abortion will be a
crime in several states,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.460
some anti-abortion advocates
are already suggesting

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.340
that states outlaw any speech
that provides information

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.080
that might be used to
terminate a pregnancy,

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.330
or encourages an illegal abortion.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.430
And there are a million thorny questions

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.930
like this that are going to come up

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.150
and it's unknown how the courts

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:19.470
are going to treat these cases.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.450
And then there is the
District of Columbia,

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.490
Washington D.C. is not a state.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.020
So now that the cord has sent abortion

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:26.010
back to the states,

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:27.930
what does that mean for D.C. residents?

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.670
Well, Washington D.C.
is a federal district

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:33.570
with limited self governance
under the 1973 Home Rule Act.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.190
However, under the Constitution,

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:38.520
Congress is the ultimate
authority over Washington D.C.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.290
So it's likely that the
current Democratic Congress

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.780
is not going to do anything
to restrict abortion in D.C.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:44.550
where it remains legal today.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.610
However, a Republican Congress
might want to end that.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.920
In fact, Representative
Andrew Klein and Republican

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.147
from Georgia said his
top focus will now be

00:12:52.147 --> 00:12:53.820
"Ending D.C.'s failed experiment

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.920
of home rule once and for all."

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.960
If Republicans retake
congressional majorities

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:59.010
later this year,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:02.130
they might decide to
criminalize abortion in D.C.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.560
And that takes us to congressional action.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.960
There are two questions before Congress.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.180
The first is could a Republican Congress

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:13.110
impose a national ban on
abortion if they take power?

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:14.940
Well, in Dobb's, the Supreme Court implied

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.770
that there was no due
process right to an abortion

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.360
that leaves decisions up to the state,

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:19.740
but we live in a federal system

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.870
with the supremacy clause.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.190
So could Congress pass a national law

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:26.340
that banned abortion everywhere
in the United States?

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:27.990
Well, a majority of the court probably

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.300
does not think the national abortion ban

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.680
would be unconstitutional.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.600
In other words, they could
probably get away with it.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.460
If Congress enacted a national ban,

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:36.720
Congress could cite their power

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.220
to regulate interstate commerce

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.210
under the Commerce Clause,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:42.330
which is often how many
different laws are passed,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:43.740
but that would be pretty easy to do.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.060
Congress could write a law that says

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.170
abortions can't be performed.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:47.140
They use any device

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:49.103
which traveled in interstate commerce,

00:13:49.103 --> 00:13:51.840
or using any medication
containing any component

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:53.430
which has traveled in interstate commerce,

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:57.270
or simply the people being
born implicates commerce

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.880
because they're going
to engage in commerce,

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.800
when they get older or
their parents engage

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:05.040
in commerce has part of the process.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:06.900
Although many of the courts
conservative justices

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:09.510
have been skeptical of the
government's commerce power.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:10.830
This situation can be different

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:12.870
if what's being regulated is abortion.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.700
Justice Roberts is the
only conservative justice

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.320
on the court who has supported

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.550
a federal government's broad right

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:18.840
to regulate interstate commerce.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.360
So his fellow justices
could wind up lobbying him

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.010
to uphold a band.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:24.420
They could also invoke section five

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:25.520
of the 14th Amendment,

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:27.263
the due process clause.

00:14:27.263 --> 00:14:28.770
This, of course,

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:29.603
is the same clause that
women once relied on

00:14:29.603 --> 00:14:31.170
for the right to abortion,

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:32.550
but Congress could pass a law stating

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:33.780
that fetuses are people

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.580
under the 14th amendment
and that they have a right

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.620
to life under that section.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.670
The second question is could
a Democratic Congress pass

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.770
a nationwide pro-abortion law

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.830
making abortion legal in 50 states?

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.170
Well, if Congress enacted
a law codifying the right

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:52.080
to an abortion nationwide,
what would happen?

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:54.900
Again, that would depend on
the way that the law is written

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.180
and whether the court would
interpret the commerce power

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:00.300
to encompass such a law, but
here I would be more skeptical.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.220
The Supreme Court could
take a narrow interpretation

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.710
of commerce power, as
we just talked about.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.390
And it's entirely possible that this court

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.223
is so outcome driven

00:15:07.223 --> 00:15:10.440
that it would allow commerce
power for a ban on abortion,

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.030
but would not allow commerce power

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.340
for a nationwide right to abortion.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:16.710
But you can't know until you try.

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:17.940
Congress could also say that women

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.790
have a due process liberty
interest to an abortion,

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:22.440
that would be advisory
to the Supreme Court,

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:23.610
they would be the ultimate arbiters,

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.410
but that would put the ball
back with the Supreme Court.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:28.710
Again, we can guess how the
Supreme Court might find

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:31.890
on that particular theory,
but again, we don't know.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:33.810
And then there's the question
of all of the other rights

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:36.210
that are implicated by
substantive due process.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.144
Could the Supreme Court start rolling back

00:15:38.144 --> 00:15:39.390
a lot of the rights

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.930
that we've come to presume
over the last 50 years?

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.990
And the answer is very
possibly those rights

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:47.880
have a target on their back:

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:49.680
contraception, interracial marriage,

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.870
intimate sexual acts, same sex marriage,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:53.940
all stem from a common judicial doctrine

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.040
notice substantive due process.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.900
Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton says,

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.250
he wants to start enforcing

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.930
the state's antiquated sodomy laws,

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:03.630
which are unconstitutional under

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:06.180
the current Supreme Court jurisprudence.

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:07.860
But Justice Thomas explicitly says,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.540
that we should do away
with all substantive

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.920
due process rights.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:13.410
Justice Kavanaugh says, the
Dobb should have zero effect

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:16.950
on other rights that originate
in substantive due process,

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:18.300
but the majority and Dobb

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:19.860
suggests that abortion is different

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.410
because it implicates potential life.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.470
But also the court engaged
in a deeply rooted analysis

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.360
that only looks to history,
and it's hard to see

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:29.820
how any of those other
rights would survive

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.020
using the same analysis

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:33.000
that the court engaged in Dobb's.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.707
And the dissenting Dobbs
made this crystal clear,

00:16:35.707 --> 00:16:38.160
"And no one should be confident
that this majority is done

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:39.090
with its work.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.030
The right Roe and Casey
recognized does not stand alone.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:44.940
To the contrary, the court
has linked it for decades

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:47.730
to other settled freedoms
involving bodily integrity,

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.250
familial relationships, and procreation.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:53.430
Most obviously, the right to
terminate a pregnancy arose out

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.510
of the right to purchase

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:55.770
and use contraception,

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.690
in turn those rights lead more recently

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.360
to rights of same sex,
intimacy and marriage.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:03.150
They are all part of the
same constitutional fabric

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.100
protecting autonomous decision making

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:07.230
over the most personal of life decisions.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.210
The majority or to be more accurate,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.400
most of it is eager to tell us today

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.160
that nothing it does cast
doubt on the precedents

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.110
that do not concern abortion.

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:17.400
But how could that be?

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.860
The lone rationale for what
the majority does today

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.690
is that the right to elect an abortion

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:23.430
is not deeply rooted in history.

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:24.263
Not until Roe,

00:17:24.263 --> 00:17:26.790
the majority argues did
people think abortion fell

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.220
within the Constitutions
guarantee of liberty.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:30.930
The same could be said though of most

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.250
of the rights the majority claims

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.780
it is not tampering with.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:35.250
The majority could write just

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:36.390
as long an opinion showing,

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:38.700
for example, that until
the mid 20th century,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.350
there was no support in American law

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:41.730
for a constitutional right

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:43.200
to obtain contraceptives.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.270
So one of two things must be true,

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:47.070
either the majority
does not really believe

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:48.120
in its own reasoning,

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:49.710
or if it does all rights

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.420
that have no history stretching back

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.180
to the mid 19th century or insecure.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.330
Either the mass of the
majority's opinion is hypocrisy,

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:59.850
or additional constitutional
rights are under threat,

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:01.590
it is one or the other.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.300
So don't be surprised
if this Supreme Court

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.640
starts rolling back
these freedoms and rights

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:08.190
and it would probably start
with same sex marriage.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:10.050
And all of that is why there are a lot

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:11.430
of reproductive health charities out there

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:12.840
that are going to need some help.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:14.520
And one very small way

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:16.710
that I like to do so is a company

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.510
that I work with called Tab For A Cause,

